WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.757
Here is the smallest provider
there could be: two patients.

00:00:03.797 --> 00:00:07.957
And here are two thousand of them.
Each pair has its own intercept,

00:00:07.997 --> 00:00:11.294
as always, and all of
them share one risk rule.

00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:16.513
We built this world, so we know
the rule: a coefficient of 0.5.

00:00:16.553 --> 00:00:21.212
Four thousand patients is a lot of
evidence. What should the fit return?

00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:25.304
It returns 0.92. Nearly twice the truth.

00:00:25.344 --> 00:00:30.022
Draw the world again, four hundred
times. The average is 1.01.

00:00:30.062 --> 00:00:33.608
This is not bad luck, and it is not a bug.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.421
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

00:00:37.461 --> 00:00:39.561
Today: too many intercepts.

00:00:41.633 --> 00:00:49.401
Look inside the pairs. In 1,224 of these
2,000, both patients had the same outcome.

00:00:49.441 --> 00:00:51.690
Episode 10 told us what happens then:

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:56.596
the intercept runs to the wall, and
the pair says nothing about the rule.

00:00:56.733 --> 00:01:00.718
In the other pairs, one patient
was readmitted and one was not.

00:01:00.758 --> 00:01:04.679
Here the intercept is free to
go wherever fits this pair best,

00:01:04.719 --> 00:01:08.582
and it settles exactly midway
between the two patients.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:09.926
That fit is too good.

00:01:09.966 --> 00:01:15.252
The intercept has been tuned to two patients
and nobody else, and it flatters them.

00:01:15.292 --> 00:01:18.782
The shared rule inherits a
little of that from every pair.

00:01:18.822 --> 00:01:21.850
For pairs the sum is exact: twice the truth.

00:01:24.533 --> 00:01:28.730
Keep the same four thousand
patients and regroup them,

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:32.344
into providers of 5, of 10, of 25, of 100.

00:01:32.384 --> 00:01:39.240
The rule comes out 26% too steep,
then 12%, then 5%, then 1%.

00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:44.852
The error shrinks as each provider grows.
It does not shrink as providers are added.

00:01:44.892 --> 00:01:48.207
More providers of the same
size bring more intercepts,

00:01:48.247 --> 00:01:50.896
and no more information for each one.

00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:56.780
And nothing in the output warns you.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:02.131
The standard error stays
small, between 0.04 and 0.08.

00:02:02.171 --> 00:02:07.549
So the 95% intervals are narrow, and in
the wrong place. With providers of 5,

00:02:07.589 --> 00:02:12.654
they hold the truth about a quarter
of the time. With pairs, never.

00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:17.618
There is a fit that escapes
this. Within each provider,

00:02:17.658 --> 00:02:22.918
take the number of readmissions as given,
and ask only which patients they were.

00:02:22.958 --> 00:02:26.118
Asked that way, the
provider's intercept cancels.

00:02:26.158 --> 00:02:30.882
It is the same move the Cox model
makes when it asks who, not when.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.792
This conditional fit gets
the rule right at every size:

00:02:34.832 --> 00:02:37.829
within 1% of the truth, even for pairs.

00:02:37.869 --> 00:02:40.940
But it has deleted the
very numbers we came for.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.089
It returns no intercepts, so
it cannot profile a provider.

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:52.621
So we keep the joint fit, and
keep an eye on provider size.

00:02:52.661 --> 00:02:58.358
At 25 patients the rule is about
5% too steep; at 100, about 1%.

00:02:58.398 --> 00:03:03.871
The software sets aside providers with
10 patients or fewer before it fits.

00:03:03.911 --> 00:03:07.766
And in our crowd, with 20
to 500 patients a provider,

00:03:07.806 --> 00:03:10.950
the rule comes out about 1% too steep.

00:03:11.067 --> 00:03:14.143
One last thing. This is
a property of the method,

00:03:14.183 --> 00:03:18.350
not of any program. Every joint
fit, in any package, has it.

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:21.429
Let's gather it up.

00:03:21.469 --> 00:03:26.314
Each intercept is fitted to its own few
patients, and overfits them a little.

00:03:26.354 --> 00:03:29.580
The shared rule inherits
that, and comes out too steep,

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.928
by a little more than one
over the provider's size.

00:03:32.968 --> 00:03:36.646
Conditioning removes the bias,
and the intercepts with it.

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:39.655
One question has been
waiting since Episode 15.

00:03:39.695 --> 00:03:44.898
When a quarter of the providers fall
outside the funnel, are they all outliers?

00:03:44.938 --> 00:03:48.424
Next time, the last
episode: wider than chance.

00:03:48.567 --> 00:03:52.672
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
